  "synopsis": [
    {
      "langue": "en",
      "text": "Rocman X (also known as Thunder Blast Man) is an unlicensed game created by Sachen, released on the Famicom and NES sometime in the 90's and also sometime later for the Game Boy Color. The NES and English Game Boy Color versions were released as Thunder Blast Man, the Chinese version of the GBC game released as Rockman X Gold, and one of the NES versions' label calls it Thunder Blaster Man.\r\nRocman X's title screen is mostly copied from the first Famicom game of Rocman's, with the logo taken from Rocman X. The game also includes a story in English where Gadem City celebrates its centennial before the mayor's daughter, Miss Lucy, is kidnapped by the Paulung-Tang gang, who then take over and sabotage the city, holding it for a $20 billion ransom. Thunder Blast Man himself (also commonly known as Rocman X) appears to be based on Rocman/Mega Man and bears a passing resemblance to Quickman from Mega Man 2, as well as sporting a cape.  His default weapon is a boomerang as opposed to a standard Mega Buster, and only one can be thrown on screen at a time. You can get different weapons as well as buy a spare continue (if you don't have one) by visiting stores located before boss fights in each world, save for the motorcycle stage. As well as this, Thunder Blast Man has a charge move triggered by holding B and, after it fully charges (designated by the health bar flashing) releasing it while holding up, left, or right to fly in that direction (releasing it while being still cancels the move.)  This allows him to get across many pits throughout the game.\r\nThe first three areas are available on the stage select screen, and most stages (save for the fourth area, where the boss is fought at the end of a single motorbike area) are split into two parts and a boss each.  After clearing these, the game becomes linear as you access the last three areas automatically in order.  The fourth area is a motorbike chase scene which looks and plays somewhat similarly to the speedboat sequence towards the end of Wave Man's stage in Mega Man 5. If you die in a stage you can continue from the point that you left off at (assuming you have a continue; you always start a new game with a continue) but if you used it up or don't have one you have to restart at the stage you're currently on, losing your bought weapons in the process.  This is a bit different in the mine cart segment of the final world, as falling into the spikes give you a game over regardless of health or continues, but you restart the level with your on-hand items (spare continue and/or weapons) intact.  The ending explains that Thunder Blast Man has saved Gadem City from the Paulung-Tang gang and that the city has made a statue of him."
    },
    {
      "langue": "fr",
      "text": "Rocman X (également connu sous le nom de Thunder Blast Man) est un jeu sans licence créé par Sachen, sorti sur Famicom et NES dans les années 1990 et aussi un peu plus tard sur Game Boy Color. Les versions anglaises NES et Game Boy Color sont sorties sous le nom de Thunder Blast Man, la version chinoise du jeu GBC est sortie sous le nom de Rockman X Gold, et l'une des versions NES l'appelle Thunder Blaster Man.\r\nL'écran titre de Rocman X est presque entièrement copié du premier jeu Famicom de Rocman, avec le logo repris de Rocman X. Le jeu comprend également une histoire en anglais dans laquelle Gadem City célèbre son centenaire avant que la fille du maire, Miss Lucy, ne soit kidnappée par le gang Paulung-Tang, qui prend alors le contrôle de la ville et la sabote en échange d'une rançon de 20 milliards de dollars."
    },
    {
      "langue": "de",
      "text": "Rocman X ist ein nicht lizenziertes Spiel von Sachen, das irgendwann in den 90er Jahren auf Famicom und NES und später auch für den Game Boy Color veröffentlicht wurde. Die englischen Versionen für NES und Game Boy Color wurden als Thunder Blast Man veröffentlicht, die chinesische Version des GBC-Spiels wurde als Rockman X veröffentlicht und ist fast vollständig von Rocmans erstem Famicom-Spiel kopiert, wobei das Logo von Rocman von den Paulung- Die Tang-Bande übernimmt daraufhin die Kontrolle über die Stadt und sabotiert sie gegen ein Lösegeld in Höhe von 20 Milliarden US-Dollar."
    },
    {
      "langue": "es",
      "text": "Rocman X (también conocido como Thunder Blast Man) es un juego sin licencia creado por Sachen, lanzado para Famicom y NES en algún momento de los años 90 y también más tarde para Game Boy Color. Las versiones de NES y de Game Boy Color fueron lanzadas como Thunder Blast Man, la versión china del juego de GBC fue lanzada como Rockman X Gold, y una de las etiquetas de la versión de NES lo llama Thunder Blaster Man. La pantalla de título de Rocman X se copia principalmente del primer juego Famicom de Rocman's, con el logotipo tomado de Rocman X. El juego también incluye una historia en inglés donde Gadem City celebra su centenario antes de que la hija del alcalde, Miss Lucy, sea secuestrada por el Paulung-Tang, que luego se hace cargo y sabotea la ciudad, reteniéndola en un rescate de $ 20 mil millones. El mismo Thunder Blast Man (también conocido comúnmente como Rocman X) parece estar basado en Rocman / Mega Man y se parece mucho a Quickman de Mega Man 2, además de lucir una capa. Su arma por defecto es un boomerang en lugar de un Mega Buster estándar, y solo se puede lanzar uno a la vez. Puedes obtener diferentes armas, así como comprar una continuación de repuesto (si no tienes una) visitando las tiendas ubicadas antes de las peleas de jefes en cada mundo, excepto para el escenario de la motocicleta. Además de esto, Thunder Blast Man tiene un movimiento de carga activado al mantener B y, después de que se cargue completamente (designado por la barra de salud parpadeando) soltándolo mientras se mantiene hacia arriba, a la izquierda o a la derecha para volar en esa dirección (soltándolo) aún cancela el movimiento.) Esto le permite atravesar muchos pozos a lo largo del juego."
    },
    {
      "langue": "it",
      "text": "Rocman X (noto anche come Thunder Blast Man) è un gioco senza licenza creato da Sachen, pubblicato per Famicom e NES negli anni '90 e successivamente anche per Game Boy Color. Le versioni inglese per NES e Game Boy Color sono state rilasciate come Thunder Blast Man, la versione cinese del gioco GBC è stata rilasciata come Rockman X Gold e una delle versioni per NES l'ha chiamata Thunder Blaster Man. La schermata del titolo di Rocman X è quasi interamente copiata da Il primo gioco Famicom di Rocman, con il logo riportato da Rocman X. Il gioco include anche una storia in lingua inglese in cui Gadem City celebra il suo centenario prima che la figlia del sindaco, Miss Lucy, venga rapita dalla banda Paulung-Tang, che poi prende controllo della città e sabotarla in cambio di un riscatto di 20 miliardi di dollari."
    },
    {
      "langue": "pt",
      "text": "Rocman X (também conhecido como Thunder Blast Man) é um jogo não licenciado criado por Sachen, lançado para Famicom e NES nos anos 90 e também algum tempo depois para Game Boy Color. As versões do NES e do Game Boy Color em inglês foram lançadas como Thunder Blast Man, a versão chinesa do jogo GBC lançada como Rockman X Gold, e uma das versões do NES o chama de Thunder Blaster Man.\r\nA tela de título de Rocman X é quase toda copiada do primeiro jogo Famicom de Rocman's, com o logotipo retirado de Rocman X. O jogo também inclui uma história em inglês onde Gadem City celebra seu centenário antes de a filha do prefeito, Miss Lucy, ser sequestrada pelos Paulung -Tang gang, que então assume e sabota a cidade, segurando-a por um resgate de $ 20 bilhões."
    }
  ],
